  "verdict": "EFFICIENCY TERM MISSING. Script 34 wrote p_UL = N_UL / n_stars with no efficiency factor, while pipeline.statistics.exclusion_curve -- the shared helper every other channel uses -- defines p_UL = N_UL / (N * efficiency(f, k)). Measured efficiency at the quoted f = 0.5066 is 0.500, because a star at exactly the threshold is scattered above it only about half the time. The published p_dark = 4.297e-04 at f >= 0.507 is therefore a factor 2.00 too strong AT THAT f. Corrected: p_dark < 8.593e-04, p_total < 1.049e-03, one star in 953 rather than 1,614. Equivalently the published number is true as stated at f >= 0.580 (90% efficient), not at f >= 0.507. BACKGROUND MODEL IS FOR THE WRONG STATISTIC. Script 34 predicts the pair tail as 2*q(T)*n_pairs with q = P(|r_star| > T) measured on single-star residuals, and applies it to |dr|, a DIFFERENCE. Observed over predicted is 0.080 at the headline row -- the prediction is 13x too large. Two candidate explanations were tested and both fail to rescue it. (a) A threshold-units mismatch: T = k*sigma_dr while q's own scale is sigma_r = 0.11338 vs sigma_dr = 0.10957, so q is evaluated at 6.76 sigma_r rather than 7. Correcting it moves obs/pred only 0.080 -> 0.094. (b) A mark-permutation null on dr, shuffling residuals within (M_Ks, distance) cells over the same pairs, over-predicts further still, obs/pred = 0.062. The residual explanation is physical: the single-star tail is dominated by contaminants COMMON to both components of a wide pair -- shared crowding, shared extinction error, shared fiducial mis-specification -- which cancel in the difference. The core already carries 53% common-mode variance and the tail evidently carries far more; the permutation destroys that cancellation entirely, which is why it over-predicts worst. CONSEQUENCE: no valid background for |dr| exists in this analysis, so `best = min(ul_cons, ul_sub)` must not take the subtracted branch. It does so in 6 of the 8 published rows, which are weakened by factors of 1.32-5.61 once forced onto the Poisson branch. The headline row (clean + bare, k = 7) is NOT among them -- it already takes the conservative branch -- so this finding costs the abstract nothing and costs the rest of the table a great deal."
}